    "finding": "Feature 'tier' is a NECESSARY CONDITION for §16 routing success: purity 1.0 (every one of the 17 genuine-success anchors satisfies 'tier ge 76.0'; ZERO successes violate it), and that same predicate excludes 0.383 of the 47-fail set. It is necessary-but-NOT-sufficient (|d|=0.74, rank-AUC 0.6796). Routing success is STRUCTURED — there is a real anchor-property frontier, not pure SGD-lottery.",
    "causation_caveat": "g3: this is CORRELATION. The necessary condition co-varies with §16's curriculum (curriculum_rank weights tier/303 at 0.30 — high-tier anchors land in later curriculum stages) and with the anchor-name distribution. Whether the feature itself CAUSES routing success, vs is a proxy for an unmeasured cause (e.g. late-stage curriculum exposure, weight-norm at that training phase), needs a controlled ablation fire. B-L3-NOTE empirical carve-out — NOT counted 🔵.",
    "implication_for_s25_candidate_D": "§25 candidate D (routing-evidence-guided expansion) has a real lever: the 'tier ge 76.0' frontier. Honest reading — because the condition is NECESSARY but not sufficient, the fail-side of the frontier (0.62 of fails are ON the success side and still fail) is where expansion can be tested: candidate D can (a) over-sample / diversify the tier-success-side fail anchors to test if more genuine content lifts routing above the necessity floor, and (b) explicitly NOT expect tier-fail-side anchors to route until the necessary condition itself is understood (it co-varies with §16's curriculum stage — see causation_caveat). The frontier is a lever for WHERE to expand, not a guarantee."
